insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 1, 'Decline curve analysis is built on rate histories rather than pressure histories mainly because:', '["Pressure has no measurable effect on rate", "Arps derived the decline family from a pressure transient solution of the diffusivity equation, so using pressure data as evidence would be circular", "Rates are metered daily as a matter of commerce, while pressure surveys are occasional and expensive", "Gauge readings above the bubble point are discarded"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A decline curve is a pressure story told in rate units, so pressure certainly matters. It is simply sampled rarely, while metered rates arrive every day because they are what gets sold. Availability, not any defect in pressure measurement, is why the discipline runs on rate-time tables.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 2, 'A pump is installed on a declining well and the oil rate jumps. Has the reservoir gained energy?', '["No: lift lowers the flowing wellbore pressure, widening drawdown at unchanged reservoir pressure", "Yes, the pump adds energy to the fluid in the drainage volume", "Yes, a higher rate can only come from higher pressure at the sandface", "No, but the decline afterwards is guaranteed to continue at exactly the same nominal decline rate as before the installation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rate is energy times plumbing. Artificial lift changes the plumbing by dropping flowing wellbore pressure, so drawdown widens while the reservoir''s stored compression is untouched. Decline then resumes from the higher rate, usually steeper, because the same store is being drained faster.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 3, 'What is the correct status of the Arps exponential, hyperbolic and harmonic curves?', '["Material balance identities for undersaturated oil", "Statistical confidence bounds around a simulation forecast", "Solutions of the diffusivity equation for a bounded reservoir", "Empirical descriptions of the shapes that production declines have historically tended to follow, with no derivation from reservoir physics behind them"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Arps catalogued the shapes real declines take and wrote down a small family of curves that reproduce them. That empirical character is both the strength, since only a rate history is needed, and the warning, since the description holds only while tomorrow keeps behaving like yesterday.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 4, 'Every decline forecast rests on one load-bearing assumption. It is that:', '["The measured rates carry random scatter that averages out over a history of sufficient length", "Operating conditions and the drive mechanism do not change", "Original oil in place has been confirmed volumetrically", "Reservoir pressure stays above the bubble point throughout"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Same completion, same choke policy, same drainage area, same drive. When any of those change, the old curve describes a well that no longer exists. Workovers, offset wells, artificial lift and waterfloods all break the assumption, which is why identifying a valid period comes before any fitting.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 5, 'What did the geoscience work hand over to this course as Ekene''s endowment?', '["A STOIIP of 12,139,208 stb", "A recoverable volume of 12,139,208 stb, obtained after applying the field''s recovery factor to oil in place", "An oil in place estimate of 121,392,080 stb over the mapped structure", "A proved developed reserves booking across the four producers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The volumetric study booked oil in place, 12.139208 MMstb, held above the 1560 m contact across the mapped cells. Oil in place is neither a recoverable volume nor a reserves booking, and turning it into what can actually be produced is the work this whole course does.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 6, 'Ekene-2 penetrated TOP_SAND at 1565 m and the field''s oil-water contact is at 1560 m. What follows?', '["The contact must be remapped to 1565 m, because a well penetration always redefines the fluid contact depth for the accumulation", "The well found oil, but the column was too thin to complete", "The well found wet sand, since its sand top lies below the contact", "The well found oil in a five metre column above the contact"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A reservoir top deeper than the contact puts the entire penetrated interval in the water leg. Ekene-2 at 1565 m and Ekene-4 at 1590 m both found wet sand on the eastern flank, and that downdip position is exactly what makes them useful as injectors from 2023-01-01.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 7, 'Ekene-5 came on production 2020-06-01 and the field started 2020-01-01. How many days separate the two dates?', '["150 days, taking each of the five intervening months as a rounded thirty day month", "151 days, because 2020 was not a leap year and February contributed 28 days", "152 days", "153 days"]'::jsonb, 2, 'January 31, February 29 in this leap year, March 31, April 30 and May 31 sum to 152. The leap day matters: dropping it gives 151. This course''s day counts are exact, because every fixture rate is evaluated at an exact day number on its own well''s clock.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 8, 'Why is it wrong to call 120 + 150 + 100 + 90 = 460 stb/d the Ekene field''s starting rate?', '["The four wells drain one tank, so their rates interfere", "On 2020-01-01 only Ekene-1 was flowing, and rates may only be added at a common calendar date with each well on its own clock", "Field rate must first be reduced by an operational uptime factor", "The initial rates are gross liquid rates, so only the oil fraction may be summed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those four initial rates belong to four different calendar dates. By the time the last producer started, the first had already been declining for 244 days. Summing rates is legitimate, but only after every well has been evaluated at one shared date.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 9, 'Ekene-1 started 2020-01-01. In the Arps formula, what value of t corresponds to its 2020-02-01 rate row?', '["t = 31 days, which reproduces the committed 115.61801032730659 stb/d", "t = 30 days, because monthly rows are conventionally spaced by an average month length", "t = 32 days", "t = 1, since the table is indexed by month number"]'::jsonb, 0, 'January has 31 days, so the second row sits exactly 31 days after that well''s t = 0. Evaluating 120 exp(-0.0012 x 31) reproduces the committed rate to the last digit, and that agreement is the check that the day count was done correctly.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 10, 'For Ekene-6, which came on production 2020-09-01, the time origin t = 0 in any decline formula is:', '["2020-01-01, the field start, so that all four producers are described on one shared timeline", "The date of the well''s peak monthly rate, which for this producer is its second reported month", "2023-01-01, the flood start", "2020-09-01, that well''s own first rate row"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each well declines from the day it starts, so t is always measured on that well''s own clock. Calendar dates are for people. One calendar date therefore corresponds to a different t for every producer, which is why multi-well work proceeds date by date rather than row by row.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 11, 'On 2023-01-01, how long had Ekene-6 been on production?', '["1096 days, the same age Ekene-1 reaches on that date, since both are counted from the field''s first oil", "852 days, counted from its own first oil", "944 days, matching Ekene-5", "1036 days"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-6 started 2020-09-01, so it reaches 852 days at the flood start. Every producer arrives at that one calendar date with a different age: 1096 days for Ekene-1, 1036 for Ekene-3, 944 for Ekene-5, and 852 for the youngest well.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 12, 'The Ekene primary window ends with which monthly rate row?', '["2025-12-01, the final row of the fixture, treating the whole record as one producing period", "2023-06-01, the last month before injected water reaches a producer", "2022-12-01, the month before injection begins", "2023-01-01, the flood start row itself"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Injection begins 2023-01-01, so the window runs from each well''s first month up to and including the month before it. The boundary is set by the change in drive mechanism, not by the month where the rate visibly turns, which happens considerably later.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 13, 'How many monthly rate rows does Ekene-6 contribute to the primary window, and why fewest of the four?', '["28 rows, because it came on stream last and all four windows end on the same date", "36 rows, matching every producer, since the window is defined by shared calendar dates", "24 rows, because the first four months of ramp-up are dropped", "31 rows, matching Ekene-5"]'::jsonb, 0, 'All four windows end on 2022-12-01 but each begins at its own first oil, so the counts run 36, 34, 31 and 28 as the start dates get later. The last well on stream has the shortest primary history and therefore the least evidence standing behind its fit.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 14, 'Ekene-1 flowed 33.4312717799524 stb/d in December 2022 and 41.22940934303498 stb/d in December 2023. What does that prove?', '["That the December 2022 rate must be a metering error", "That the well switched to harmonic decline, the flattest member of the family", "That the fitted exponent b rose between the two years while the model stayed valid", "That no single Arps curve can describe both years, because every member of the family decreases at every instant"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A rate 23 percent higher a year later is not a slower decline; it is a rise. Exponential, hyperbolic and harmonic forms are monotonically decreasing by construction, so a history that turns upward has left the family, and the drive mechanism is what changed.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-declines-and-the-field', 15, 'Across the six monthly rows from 2023-01-01, Ekene-1 reports 32.210476049246076 stb/d every month. What is that flat stretch?', '["The economic limit being enforced as a reporting floor until shut-in", "The lag before injected water reaches the producer", "A metering plateau from the test separator being offline for the first half of 2023", "Harmonic decline, which becomes indistinguishable from a constant rate after three years on production"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Injected water needs time to travel through the rock before it supports a producer. During that lag the rate neither falls nor rises, and afterwards it climbs to a peak in December 2023. A rate holding exactly constant is already outside the Arps family, which never has zero slope.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 1, 'What is the defining property of an exponential decline?', '["The well loses the same number of barrels per day each day", "The rate loses the same fraction of itself in equal time steps", "The cumulative production grows linearly with time on production", "The decline rate falls in proportion to the rate, so the curve flattens steadily as the well ages"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Constant fractional loss, not constant volume loss, is what makes a decline exponential. Early on that fraction is many barrels per day of lost capacity and late in life it is a trickle, yet the fraction itself never changes. A decline that eases off belongs to another member of the family.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 2, 'Ekene-1 has Di = 0.0012 per day, giving a half-life of ln(2)/Di = 577.622650466621 days. How does that half-life behave later in the well''s life?', '["It lengthens, because each successive halving starts from a smaller rate and therefore takes longer to achieve", "It shortens once the rate drops below half the initial rate", "It only applies while the rate is above the economic limit", "It stays at 577.622650466621 days for every halving"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For an exponential well the time from 120 to 60 stb/d, from 60 to 30 and from 30 to 15 is identical, because the fractional decline never changes. A halving time that lengthens is the harmonic signature, and the economic limit is a booking cutoff that has no effect on the curve itself.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 3, 'Ekene-5 is harmonic with qi = 100 stb/d and Di = 0.0015 per day. What is its rate after one year?', '["64.6203554119548 stb/d", "57.7500000000000 stb/d, obtained by applying the annual nominal decline of 0.5475 as a direct haircut on qi", "77.4390939428753 stb/d, the same one-year rate the exponential producer reaches", "80.5055750110695 stb/d"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The harmonic form needs one multiplication, one addition and one division: 1 + 0.0015 x 365 = 1.5475, then 100 divided by 1.5475. Subtracting a nominal fraction is never a valid shortcut, and the other rates quoted belong to different producers with different parameters.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 4, 'Ekene-5 first halves, from 100 to 50 stb/d, at t = 666.666666666667 days. When does it reach 25 stb/d?', '["At 1333.33 days, since each halving takes the same time", "At 1155.25 days", "At 2000 days, because the second halving takes twice as long as the first one did", "Never, because a harmonic decline approaches a nonzero terminal rate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reaching a quarter of qi needs 1 + Di t = 4, so t = 3/Di = 2000 days, which is 1333.3 days after the first halving. Each successive halving takes twice as long as the one before, and the harmonic curve does fall to zero eventually, just extremely slowly.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 5, 'Ekene-3 has qi = 150 stb/d, Di = 0.002 per day and b = 0.5. What does its rate equation simplify to?', '["150 divided by the square root of (1 + 0.001 t), because the exponent 1/b applies as a root rather than a power", "150 divided by (1 + 0.001 t) squared", "150 times exp(-0.001 t), since a b of one half halves the effective decline constant", "150 divided by (1 + 0.002 t) squared"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With b = 0.5 the bracket is 1 + b Di t = 1 + 0.001 t and the power 1/b is exactly 2, so the formula becomes a plain square in the denominator. At t = 730 days that gives 1.73 squared, 2.9929, and 150 divided by 2.9929 is 50.1186140532594 stb/d.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 6, 'For Ekene-6, with b = 0.35, evaluating the hyperbolic rate equation at one year requires:', '["Squaring the bracket, since any b below one half rounds to an exponent of two", "Nothing beyond addition, because b below one half removes the power entirely", "The natural logarithm of the bracket, which replaces the power when b is not a simple fraction", "Raising 1.12775 to the power 1/b = 2.85714285714286, which gives 1.40987149752878"]'::jsonb, 3, 'b Di t is 0.35 x 0.001 x 365 = 0.12775, so the bracket is 1.12775 and the exponent 1/b is 2.857142857. Dividing 90 by 1.40987149752878 gives 63.8356049879382 stb/d. Only b = 0.5 yields the convenient square; every other b needs the power key.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 7, 'Two wells share the same qi and the same Di but one has a higher b. Which is producing more at every time after t = 0?', '["The well with the higher b, at every single time thereafter", "The well with the lower b, since a larger b denotes a steeper decline", "Neither, because b affects only the cumulative and not the rate", "They cross: the higher b well is below early and above late, which is why b cannot be read from early data alone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'b measures how quickly a decline brakes itself, so a larger b means a slower fade, a fatter tail and a larger ultimate recovery from identical early data. The curves never cross, and reading a high b as a fast decline is the most common misreading of a fit report.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 8, 'Why do fitting engines treat exponential decline as a separate model rather than as the hyperbolic form with b = 0?', '["Because a b of zero would make the bracket negative for large t", "Because the exponential form has three parameters while the hyperbolic has two", "Because 1/b is undefined at b = 0, so exponential is a limiting case rather than a substitution", "Because Arps published the exponential form decades before the hyperbolic family was proposed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Letting b shrink toward zero drives the bracket toward 1 while the power runs off to infinity, and the limit of that competition is qi exp(-Di t). You cannot reach it by substitution, since dividing by zero is undefined, so the exponential is coded as a model in its own right.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 9, 'The panel reports Ekene-3''s fitted b as 0.49999999999999994 rather than 0.5. What does that tell you?', '["That the teaching data carries a small amount of measurement noise", "That the b grid is stepped by repeated addition of 0.05 in binary floating point, so the tile shows the raw accumulated value", "That the fit failed to converge and should be repeated with a finer tolerance", "That the well is very slightly steeper than a true b of one half"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine scans b in 0.05 steps built up by repeated addition, and binary floating point cannot represent 0.05 exactly, so the accumulated value lands a few units in the last place away from the decimal. Read it as one half; the Ekene data itself is noise-free.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 10, 'Ekene-1 has a nominal decline of 0.438 per year. What is wrong with computing its one-year rate as 120 x (1 - 0.438) = 67.44 stb/d?', '["Nothing is wrong, provided the result is later corrected for the days in a leap year", "The nominal decline should have been converted from 0.0012 per day using 360 days rather than 365", "The subtraction is right but the initial rate should have been the second table row rather than 120", "The nominal decline is an instantaneous rate of fractional loss, not a full-year haircut, and exponentiating gives 77.4390939428753 stb/d"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The well slows its losses continuously as the rate falls, so applying the instantaneous rate for a whole year overstates the damage by about 10 stb/d here. The honest conversion runs through the exponential, and the resulting annual fraction is what the effective decline reports.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 11, 'Ekene-5 has Di = 0.0015 per day. What is its tangent effective annual decline?', '["0.421606010621992", "0.547500000000000, which is the nominal decline per year expressed as a fraction", "0.353796445880452, the fraction of the initial rate this well has actually lost after one year", "0.305803349122021"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tangent conversion is 1 - exp(-Di x 365) = 1 - exp(-0.5475) = 0.421606010621992. Restating the nominal figure as a fraction is not a conversion at all, the secant convention gives a different and smaller number for this curved well, and 0.3058 belongs to another producer.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 12, 'For which Ekene producer do the tangent and secant effective annual declines come out identical, and why?', '["Ekene-5, because a harmonic well loses a constant fraction of its rate each year", "Ekene-6, because its b of 0.35 is small enough for the two conventions to agree exactly", "Ekene-1, because it is exponential", "None of them, since the two conventions differ by construction for every possible decline curve"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tangent conversion assumes the well keeps declining exponentially at its initial rate of loss, which for a genuinely exponential well is exactly what happens: both routes give 0.354674217142705. Any curvature makes the true one-year drop smaller than the tangent figure.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 13, 'SPEE REP #6 Table 1 pairs a nominal decline of 10 percent per year with which secant effective decline at b = 1?', '["10.517092 percent, the nominal figure compounded continuously over the year", "9.090909 percent", "9.516258 percent", "9.090909 percent, but only for wells whose fitted b lies strictly between 0 and 1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For a harmonic well the secant value is 1 - 1/(1 + 0.10) = 0.0909090909090909. The 9.51625819640405 percent figure in the same table is the tangent effective decline, which applies to the exponential conversion, and the secant definition is not restricted to b below one.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 14, 'On a semilog plot of Ekene-1 (Di = 0.0012 per day), how far does the line fall per year?', '["0.438 log cycles, since the nominal annual decline is read directly off the log axis", "0.354674217142705 log cycles, the effective annual decline expressed in log units", "0.301029995663981 log cycles, because the well halves roughly once a year", "0.190220983073624 log cycles per year, which is Di x 365 divided by ln(10)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Taking base ten logarithms of qi exp(-Di t) gives a straight line whose slope is Di divided by ln(10) = 2.30258509299405. Annualised, 0.438/2.30258509299405 = 0.190220983073624, and the same value appears as log10(120) minus log10(77.4390939428753).'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-three-models', 15, 'A well''s semilog rate history bends upward, away from a straight line. What does that mean?', '["The rate is still falling, but more slowly than an exponential would", "The rate has begun to rise again, so support has arrived from outside the well", "The rate is falling faster than any member of the Arps family allows and something has changed", "The plot uses cumulative production on the horizontal axis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Upward bending, or concavity upward, is the signature of a shrinking instantaneous decline, which is what hyperbolic and harmonic wells do. The rate itself still decreases at every instant. Genuine support shows as the line turning around, which no Arps curve can reproduce.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 1, 'For a well with initial rate qi and nominal decline Di, the harmonic cumulative production is:', '["Np = (qi/Di)(1 - exp(-Di t))", "Np = (qi/Di) ln(1 + Di t), with Di and t sharing a time unit", "Np = (qi/Di) ln(Di t)", "Np = qi/(Di(1 - b)) times (1 - (1 + b Di t)^(1 - 1/b)), evaluated at b = 1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Integrating the harmonic rate qi/(1 + Di t) produces a logarithm of one plus Di t, scaled by qi/Di. An exponential bracket belongs to the exponential model, dropping the 1 inside the logarithm gives a negative quantity for small t, and the hyperbolic expression divides by (1 - b), which is zero at b = 1.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 2, 'Ekene-5 is harmonic with qi = 100 stb/d and Di = 0.0015 per day. Its cumulative production at t = 365 days is:', '["49303.5702249401 stb", "29109.3817051076 stb", "58807.5520048379 stb", "66666.6666666667 stb"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Di t = 0.5475, ln(1.5475) = 0.436640725576614, and 66666.6666666667 times that bracket gives 29109.3817051076 stb. The rejected values are this well''s cumulative at 730 days, its cumulative on the day the flood starts, and the scale factor qi/Di itself.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 3, 'For the harmonic model, qi/Di is described as a scale rather than a ceiling. The practical consequence is that:', '["Np approaches 66666.6666666667 stb for Ekene-5 and never passes it, exactly as an exponential ceiling behaves", "Np keeps climbing without bound as time runs on, because ln(1 + Di t) has no upper limit of its own", "Np grows without bound only while Di exceeds 1 per day, and settles onto a limit otherwise", "Np stops climbing once the rate falls to the 10 stb/d economic limit, which the closed form applies internally"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The exponential bracket saturates at 1, which is what makes qi/Di a ceiling there. The harmonic bracket is a logarithm, and a logarithm grows forever, so nothing inside the closed form ever stops the volume. Only an economic limit imposed from outside stops it, and the formula knows nothing about that limit.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 4, 'The hyperbolic cumulative carries the power term (1 + b Di t) raised to the exponent 1 - 1/b. For Ekene-6, where b = 0.35, that exponent is:', '["1.85714285714286, the same magnitude carrying the opposite sign", "-2.85714285714286", "-1.85714285714286", "-1.00000000000000"]'::jsonb, 2, '1/0.35 is 2.85714285714286, so 1 - 1/b evaluates to -1.85714285714286. The exponent is negative for every b between 0 and 1, which is what makes the power term shrink toward zero and the cumulative climb toward its leading factor. A positive exponent would send the bracket negative and the volume with it. The value -2.85714285714286 is -1/b, and -1 is the exponent for Ekene-3, where b = 0.5.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 5, 'Ekene-3 has qi = 150 stb/d, Di = 0.002 per day and b = 0.5. The leading factor qi/(Di(1 - b)) of its cumulative equals:', '["75000 stb", "76326.1296660118 stb", "150000 stb", "66666.6666666667 stb"]'::jsonb, 2, '0.002 multiplied by (1 - 0.5) is 0.001, and 150 divided by 0.001 is 150000 stb. Because the exponent 1 - 1/b is negative for b below 1, the power term decays and the cumulative climbs toward this leading factor, which is a genuine ceiling for a hyperbolic well with b less than one. The rejected values are qi/Di, the volume produced by the flood date, and the harmonic well''s scale factor.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 6, 'Why must the hyperbolic cumulative be replaced by the logarithmic form when b = 1?', '["Because the grid search skips any b within 0.001 of 1, so no hyperbolic parameters exist at that value", "Because harmonic wells decline too slowly for the power form to converge over a 36 month window", "Because the exponent 1 - 1/b turns positive at b = 1, which drives the bracket and the volume negative", "Because the leading factor divides by (1 - b), and that denominator is exactly zero when b reaches 1"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At b = 1 the denominator Di(1 - b) vanishes and the expression is undefined. The harmonic logarithm is the limit of the hyperbolic cumulative as b approaches 1, which is why the engine carries harmonic as a model in its own right rather than as a hyperbolic special case. The exponent at b = 1 is zero, not positive.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 7, 'Ekene-3''s cumulative at t = 365 days is 40109.8901098901 stb, a value whose digits visibly repeat. The correct reading is:', '["The repeating digits mark the point where floating point arithmetic began to cycle, so the value should be truncated to 40109.89 stb before use", "The value is exact: with b = 0.5 the exponent is -1, so the volume reduces to the rational number 54750/1.365", "The value was rounded to 15 figures from an irrational result, and the apparent repeat is a coincidence of that rounding", "The repeat shows the fixture row was typed by hand, so the cumulative should be recomputed from the monthly rates"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At b = 0.5 the exponent 1 - 1/b is exactly -1, so the power term is a plain reciprocal and the whole cumulative collapses to a ratio of two rational numbers. Rational numbers have repeating decimal expansions, and the engine, the fixture and a calculator all agree on these digits.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 8, 'Ekene-6 came on production 2020-09-01. In its own decline clock, the flood date of 2023-01-01 sits at:', '["t = 1096 days", "t = 1036 days", "t = 852 days", "t = 944 days"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Time in an Arps evaluation runs from the well''s own start of production, not from a field calendar. Ekene-6 started eight months after Ekene-1, so the same calendar date sits 244 days earlier in its decline clock. The rejected values are the flood date on the clocks of Ekene-1, Ekene-3 and Ekene-5, and feeding every well the same t is a quiet way to overstate the younger ones.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 9, 'Ekene-6 (qi 90 stb/d, Di 0.001 per day, b 0.35) had produced how much oil when the waterflood began?', '["27706.7638074657 stb", "53183.4217032003 stb", "62699.3674977314 stb", "138461.538461538 stb"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With b Di t = 0.2982, the power term (1.2982)^-1.85714285714286 is 0.615897509921331, and 138461.538461538 multiplied by 0.384102490078669 gives 53183.4217032003 stb. The rejected values are the well''s one year cumulative, its cumulative at t = 1096 days, which belongs to Ekene-1''s clock, and the leading factor it never reaches.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 10, 'Ekene-1 opens at 120 stb/d and January 2020 has 31 days. The snapshot volume of 120 times 31 = 3720 stb compares with the exact 3651.65806057785 stb as:', '["an overstatement of 68.3419394221546 stb, which is 1.87153173403480 percent of the exact volume", "an understatement of 68.3419394221546 stb, because the falling curve sits above the rectangle", "an overstatement of 2.19143303530745 stb, which is the error a rectangular sum makes on this month", "an exact match, once the rate is read as the average rate for the month rather than the rate on the 1st"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Holding the 1 January rate for the whole month credits every later day with more oil than it produced, so the snapshot runs high. The 2.19143303530745 stb figure is the much smaller error left when the same month is sliced into 31 daily rectangles instead of being treated as one.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 11, 'A rectangular sum takes each interval''s height from the rate at the START of that interval. Applied to any declining rate history, this sum will:', '["Understate the true volume, because each rectangle then sits below the falling curve", "Overstate the true volume, whatever decline model the rates happen to follow", "Overstate for exponential declines and understate for harmonic and hyperbolic ones", "Match the true volume exactly, provided every interval is given the same number of days"]'::jsonb, 1, 'If the rate only falls, the value at the start of an interval is the highest value in it, so every rectangle sits above the curve and the sum exceeds the area. The argument uses nothing but the direction of change, so it holds for all three Arps models and for messy real data as well.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 12, 'Ekene-1''s monthly snapshot sum comes to 74502.9269694921 stb against an exact primary cumulative of 73157.9366256283 stb, while the engine''s daily forecast sum runs 0.0682290617407998 percent BELOW the closed-form EUR. The signs differ because:', '["the snapshot works in stock tank barrels while the forecast engine accumulates reservoir barrels before converting", "the snapshot covers 36 months of primary production while the forecast runs on to the 10 stb/d economic limit", "the snapshot takes each rectangle''s height at the start of its interval, while the daily sum takes each height at the end", "the snapshot uses the planted parameters while the engine uses fitted ones, and fitted parameters are slightly low"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Heights taken at interval starts put every rectangle above a falling curve, and heights taken at interval ends put every rectangle below it. One method therefore has to run high and the other low. Both fits recover the planted parameters exactly on this noise-free data, so parameter error plays no part.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 13, 'Slicing January 2020 into 31 daily rectangles instead of one monthly rectangle moves the error from 68.3419394221546 stb to 2.19143303530745 stb. The general rule this illustrates is:', '["narrower intervals shrink the error of a rectangular sum roughly in proportion to their width, without ever removing it", "a rectangular sum becomes exact once the interval is one day, the resolution at which rates are measured", "the error falls with the square of the number of intervals, so 31 slices cut it by a factor near 961", "monthly sums are biased because months differ in length, while daily sums are unbiased because days do not"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Thirty-one narrow rectangles hug the curve far better than one wide one, and cutting the interval by a factor of 31 cut the error by about the same factor. That is why an engine''s daily sum is a nuisance in a distant decimal place while a monthly snapshot is a real booking error, and why no finite slicing is ever exact.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 14, 'January 2020''s snapshot overstates Ekene-1 by 1.87153173403480 percent and February 2020''s by 1.75009179630883 percent. The percentages differ because:', '["February 2020 has 29 days, so the opening rate is held constant over a shorter stretch of the month", "February''s opening rate of 115.618010327307 stb/d is lower, and lower rates always carry smaller relative errors", "the decline steepens as the well ages, so each successive month carries a smaller relative error than the one before it", "February''s exact volume of 3295.25235830158 stb was obtained from the harmonic closed form rather than the exponential one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For an exponential decline the relative overstatement depends only on how long the rate is held constant, so every 31 day month repeats 1.87153173403480 percent and the 29 day month of a leap February comes in lower. March 2020, another 31 day month, returns to the same percentage as January.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-cumulative-volumes', 15, 'The engine prints its daily-sum cumulative for Ekene-1 as 91604.1233600709 stb, fifteen significant figures. What does that precision establish about the number''s accuracy?', '["It bounds the error at about 1e-15 stb, the resolution implied by the digits that were printed", "It shows the sum has converged, because a rectangular sum only prints its full digit string once it has", "Nothing at all: the value is still 0.0682290617407998 percent below the exact volume of the same forecast", "It confirms agreement with the closed form to 15 figures, since both are built from the same qi and Di"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Precision is how finely a number is written and accuracy is how close it is to the truth, and the two are independent. This sum is written to fifteen figures and is still short of the exact volume by 62.5433065957332 stb, because the method carries a bias that no amount of printed detail can remove.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 1, 'Which straight-line relationship does the engine regress when it fits an exponential decline?', '["ln q against t, a straight line whose slope is -Di and whose intercept is ln qi", "Rate against ln t, with Di read from that slope and qi from the intercept", "q against t, taking Di from the slope divided by the mean rate over the window", "ln q against ln t, whose slope is -Di and whose intercept is the logarithm of the initial rate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Taking logarithms of q = qi exp(-Di t) gives ln q = ln qi - Di t, which is linear in time. Least squares solves that in one step. Plotting rate against a logarithm of time, or logarithm against logarithm, describes other decline families and does not linearise this one.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 2, 'The harmonic linearisation regresses 1/q against t. What do the slope and intercept of that line carry?', '["Intercept qi and slope Di, both read straight off the regression without further work", "Intercept 1/qi and slope Di/qi, so that Di needs the recovered qi before it can be read off", "Intercept ln qi and slope -Di, exactly as in the exponential case but on reciprocal axes", "Intercept 1/qi and slope Di, so that Di is obtained without reference to the intercept"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Inverting q = qi/(1 + Di t) gives 1/q = 1/qi + (Di/qi) t. The intercept therefore returns qi by reciprocal, and Di comes from the slope multiplied by that recovered qi. Reading Di from the slope alone would be wrong by a factor of qi.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 3, 'The hyperbolic transform q^(-b) is linear in time only if b is already known. How does the engine handle that?', '["It solves for b with a Newton iteration on the misfit, then runs a single regression at the converged value", "It fixes b at 0.5, the midpoint of the family, and reports any departure as a warning", "It estimates b from the ratio of the early rate to the late rate, then regresses once", "It walks b across a grid in steps of 0.05, regresses at every candidate, and keeps the best"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The exponent sits inside the transform, so it cannot come out of the same regression. The engine sidesteps that by trying candidate values in fixed increments and running the whole linear fit at each one, then keeping the candidate whose curve reproduces the rates best. Nothing is solved iteratively and nothing is fixed in advance.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 4, 'Auto-Select ranks the three families on RMSE rather than on the quality tier. Why does that choice matter?', '["Because the quality tier saturates: several fits of very different accuracy can all report Excellent", "Because RMSE is dimensionless, which makes it comparable across wells of different size", "Because the tier is computed only after a model has been chosen, so it is unavailable at selection time", "Because RMSE rewards the model with the fewest parameters, which breaks ties in favour of the exponential"]'::jsonb, 0, 'On a three year window every Arps family can land above R2 = 0.95, so the tier stops discriminating exactly when a decision is needed. RMSE keeps counting in barrels per day and separates fits the tier calls identical. RMSE carries units, and it has no preference for simpler models.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 5, 'In every fit the engine reports, the time origin t = 0 sits at:', '["the field''s start of production on 2020-01-01, so that all four Ekene wells share one clock", "the earliest data point inside the fitted window, wherever that window happens to begin", "the earliest row in the well''s rate table, whether or not the fitted window includes it", "the month in which the well reached its maximum rate, which anchors qi at the observed peak"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clock starts where the fitted data starts, so the reported qi is the model rate at the beginning of the window rather than the well''s opening rate. Fit a window that begins later and qi means something different, which is the check to run whenever a fitted qi looks surprising.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 6, 'Ekene-1 reads 120 stb/d on 2020-01-01 and 115.618010327307 stb/d on 2020-02-01, 31 days later. The logarithms are 4.787491742782046 and 4.750291742782046. The two-point decline is:', '["0.0372 per day, the drop in the logarithms taken directly as the decline", "0.0012 per day", "0.00120000000000000 per day, but only after dividing by ln(120) to normalise the drop", "0.001200000 per year, since a drop measured between calendar months is an annual quantity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The logarithms differ by 0.0372 over 31 days, and 0.0372 divided by 31 is 0.0012 per day, which is exactly the value planted in the fixture. The drop itself is not a rate of decline until it is divided by the elapsed time, and no normalising by ln qi belongs in the calculation.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 7, 'The Auto-Select fit of Ekene-1''s primary window returns RMSE = 1.42601125915484e-14 stb/d. The right way to describe that number is:', '["the accumulated rounding of floating point arithmetic on data that lies exactly on its planted curve", "the smallest misfit the engine is able to report, since values below it are clipped to zero", "an unusually good fit of measured field rates, of the standard a careful analyst should aim for", "the standard error of the fitted qi, reported in the same units as the rate for convenience"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A misfit fourteen orders of magnitude below the data is not a statement about the well or about the analyst. The Ekene rates were planted on exact Arps curves, so the only residual left is what the arithmetic itself cannot represent, and real rate histories never produce it.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 8, 'Why does the engine recover Ekene-1''s planted qi and Di to the digit rather than merely coming close?', '["The engine detects planted fixtures and returns their parameters directly, bypassing the regression", "The grid search refines Di until the residual falls below a tolerance built into the fitting routine", "The data is noise-free, the linearisation is exact, and least squares on a perfect line returns it", "Auto-Select averages the three families, and the two wrong ones cancel out on clean data"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Three things line up. Every rate sits exactly on the planted curve, the logarithmic transform of an exponential is an identity rather than an approximation, and a regression through collinear points reproduces the line exactly. Nothing is detected, refined or averaged.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 9, 'R2 is defined as 1 - SSE/SST. What is SST?', '["The sum of squared residuals of the fitted curve, which is why R2 falls as the fit worsens", "The sum of squared deviations of the measured rates about their own mean over the window", "The total production over the fitted window, which normalises the residuals to a volume", "The sum of squared differences between successive monthly rates, a measure of how fast the well declines"]'::jsonb, 1, 'SST measures how much the data varies about its own mean, which is the benchmark a flat line at that mean would achieve. R2 therefore reports the share of that variation the fitted curve accounts for. The squared residuals are SSE, the other term in the ratio.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 10, 'A four month fit misses by residuals of +1, -1, -1 and +1 stb/d on rates near 90 stb/d, giving RMSE = 1 stb/d and R2 = 0.985004686035614. What does the pairing of those two statistics show?', '["R2 near 0.985 corresponds to a misfit near 1.5 percent, a conversion that holds for any rate history", "RMSE and R2 disagree here, so at least one of them has been computed on the transformed scale", "R2 flatters the fit, because a declining well varies so much about its own mean that SST is large", "R2 is the more reliable of the two, since it is dimensionless and therefore free of unit errors"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A miss of about one barrel a day in ninety is roughly a one percent error, yet R2 reads 0.985 because it is scored against the weak benchmark of a flat line at the mean rate. There is no fixed conversion between the two statistics, and both are computed on the original rate scale.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 11, 'getFitQuality returns a tier along with the R2, the RMSE and the point count it was given. Which of those inputs decides the tier?', '["R2 and RMSE together, with RMSE breaking ties between fits in the same R2 band", "R2 alone", "RMSE alone, expressed as a percentage of the mean rate over the fitted window", "R2 and the point count, so that a fit on 36 monthly rows outranks the same R2 on 12 rows"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The banding compares R2 against 0.95, 0.90 and 0.80 and nothing else. RMSE and the number of points travel with the result for the analyst to read, but they never move the tier, so two fits missing by one barrel a day and by twenty can carry the same word.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 12, 'The engine reports R2 as exactly 0 for a fit that is worse than a flat line at the mean rate. What is happening?', '["The regression failed to converge, and zero is the placeholder the engine returns in that case", "SST came out as zero, which makes the ratio undefined and the reported R2 zero by convention", "R2 is clamped at zero by the engine, so a genuinely negative value is reported at that floor", "The fitted curve crossed the data an even number of times, cancelling the residual sum to zero"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The definition allows R2 to go below zero whenever SSE exceeds SST, but the engine takes the maximum of zero and the computed value before reporting it. An R2 of exactly 0.00 is therefore a floor rather than a coincidence, and it means the curve is no better than quoting the average rate.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 13, 'Forcing an exponential model on Ekene-3''s hyperbolic primary window returns R2 = 0.987334417750128, and forcing a harmonic model returns R2 = 0.951593684935824. Both land in the Excellent band. The lesson is that:', '["Ekene-3''s data must contain noise, since a clean hyperbolic well would reject both wrong models outright", "the Excellent band is set too low and should be raised to 0.99 before either fit is judged", "a three year window of clean data does not carry enough shape for R2 to identify the family", "the two wrong models are acceptable substitutes here, because a fit above 0.95 reproduces the history"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both forced fits are provably wrong about a well whose planted parameters are known, and the banding cannot tell them from a fit that recovers those parameters to fourteen decimal places. The data is noise-free, and reproducing a short history is not the same as identifying the model that generated it.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 14, 'The forced exponential fit of Ekene-3 returns qi = 137.390666611994 stb/d, well below the well''s actual opening rate of 150 stb/d. Why does least squares settle there?', '["Because the exponential family cannot bend the way the data does, so the fit compromises across the window", "Because the exponential transform discards the earliest point, which leaves the opening month unfitted", "Because qi is reported at the midpoint of the window rather than at its start for forced models", "Because 137.390666611994 is the mean of the opening rate and the closing rate of the primary window"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A hyperbolic well is steep early and flat late, and a single exponential cannot be both. Least squares splits the difference, which pulls the intercept down and leaves a misfit that runs below the data early, above it through the middle, and below it again at the end. Every point in the window is fitted, and qi always sits at the window start.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-fitting-the-data', 15, 'Ten years out, the forced harmonic fit of Ekene-3 predicts 12.7052238195246 stb/d and the forced exponential fit predicts 0.945193823374231 stb/d, against a true 6.93721817551162 stb/d. What does this spread establish?', '["That the harmonic fit is the safer of the two, since booking the higher tail is the conservative choice", "That the forecasts diverge only because ten years lies outside the range the closed forms are valid over", "That both wrong models drift because their Di values, 0.00359325039110851 and 0.00136416266227741, differ from the true 0.002 per day", "That model choice, which R2 could not make, controls the tail where the reserves live"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Three curves that agree to within a few barrels a day across the fitted history disagree by more than a factor of thirteen a decade later. The closed forms remain valid throughout, booking a higher tail is optimistic rather than conservative, and the differing Di values are a symptom of the wrong family rather than an independent cause.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 1, 'Where does the economic limit that defines a well''s EUR come from?', '["From a price, an operating cost and a fiscal split, which is why two operators holding the same well can book different EURs", "From the reservoir''s abandonment pressure, read off the most recent pressure survey", "From the fitted decline model, at the rate where the Arps equation stops being valid", "From a regulatory floor rate published for each producing basin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The limit is the rate at which revenue and operating cost break even, so it is built from commercial inputs rather than from the rock or the curve. Two operators with different cost structures can book different EURs from one decline and both be right.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 2, 'A well costs 400 dollars a day to operate, oil sells for 50 dollars a barrel, and the operator keeps 80 percent of that after royalty and tax. What is its economic limit?', '["10 stb/d, dividing 400 by the 40 dollars a barrel the operator actually nets", "8 stb/d, dividing the daily operating cost by the posted 50 dollar price", "6.4 stb/d, applying the 80 percent net share to the daily cost before dividing by the price", "16 stb/d, dividing the daily operating cost by half of the posted oil price"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The net revenue per barrel is 50 times 0.8, which is 40 dollars. Dividing the 400 dollar daily cost by 40 dollars a barrel gives a break-even rate of 10 stb/d, the working limit used throughout the Ekene examples.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 3, 'Why can a harmonic well''s EUR not be obtained by integrating its decline curve to infinity?', '["The harmonic rate turns negative once the product Di t rises above one", "The integral of qi/(1 + Di t) diverges, so only the limit makes the volume finite", "Harmonic declines are only considered valid over the first year of production after the ramp", "The engine''s forecast routine stops after a fixed number of daily steps and so never reaches infinity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The area under the harmonic rate curve grows without bound, so a harmonic well produces an infinite volume on paper. Ekene-5 illustrates it: its cumulative is 153505.672866270 stb at 6000 days and still climbing at 300723.967101123 stb by 60000 days. The economic limit is what makes the booking finite.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 4, 'The exponential EUR formula, EUR = (qi - qLimit)/Di, is really just:', '["The area of a triangle whose base is 1/Di and whose height is qi minus the limit rate", "The ceiling qi/Di, corrected for the days each year that the well spends shut in", "The exponential cumulative Np = (qi - q)/Di, evaluated at the limit rate", "An empirical approximation that holds only while the product Di t stays below one"]'::jsonb, 2, 'EUR is the cumulative production evaluated at the moment the rate reaches the limit. For an exponential decline the cumulative equals the rate drop divided by the decline, so substituting the limit rate for the current rate turns the cumulative into the ultimate.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 5, 'Ekene-1''s primary fit returns qi 120 stb/d and Di 0.0012 per day. Its EUR at the 10 stb/d limit is:', '["83333.3333333333 stb, the booking a 20 stb/d limit would have produced", "100000 stb, which is the ratio of qi to Di", "95833.3333333333 stb, which is what the same well books at a 5 stb/d limit", "91666.6666666667 stb, from 110 divided by 0.0012"]'::jsonb, 3, 'One subtraction and one division: 120 minus 10 is 110 stb/d, and 110 divided by 0.0012 per day is 91666.6666666667 stb. The other figures are real Ekene-1 numbers in the wrong roles, being the ceiling and the bookings at the 5 and 20 stb/d limits.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 6, 'For Ekene-1 the ceiling qi/Di is 100000 stb while the EUR at 10 stb/d is 91666.6666666667 stb. What does the difference represent?', '["8333.33333333333 stb lying below the limit, left in the reservoir because lifting them loses money", "The volume produced during the waterflood era after 2023-01-01", "The discretization gap between a daily forecast sum and the closed form", "The share of the booking attributable to the well''s hyperbolic tail"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ceiling is what an exponential well would deliver if produced forever at no cost. The EUR stops at the limit, so 8.33333333333333 percent of the ceiling stays in the ground. Ekene-1 is exponential and has no hyperbolic tail at all.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 7, 'Ekene-5 is harmonic with qi 100 stb/d and Di 0.0015 per day. Booked at a 10 stb/d limit, its EUR is:', '["66666.6666666667 stb, which is simply qi divided by Di", "153505.672866270 stb, from 66666.6666666667 multiplied by ln 10", "199715.484903599 stb, which is what this well books at a 5 stb/d limit instead", "666666.666666667 stb, from qi divided by Di and then scaled by the ratio of qi to the limit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The harmonic EUR is (qi/Di) ln(qi/qLimit). Here qi/Di is 66666.6666666667 and ln(100/10) is 2.30258509299405, and the product is 153505.672866270 stb. The logarithm is the whole point: without it the fat harmonic tail would never be tallied correctly.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 8, 'Ekene-3 has qi 150 stb/d, Di 0.002 per day and b 0.5. In its hyperbolic EUR, the bracket term qi^(1-b) minus qLimit^(1-b) at a 10 stb/d limit evaluates to:', '["14.1650849510256, which belongs to Ekene-6 and its exponent of 0.65", "9.08517105374751, the difference of the two square roots", "111270.166537926, which is the finished EUR rather than the bracket", "12.2474487139159, which is the square root of 150 taken on its own"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With b of 0.5, both powers are square roots: 12.2474487139159 minus 3.16227766016838 gives 9.08517105374751. Multiplying by the leading constant 12247.4487139159 completes the booking at 111270.166537926 stb.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 9, 'In the hyperbolic time to limit, t = ((qi/qLimit)^b - 1)/(b Di), what is Ekene-6''s denominator, given b 0.35 and Di 0.001 per day?', '["0.0035 per day, from the product with the decimal carried one place wrong", "0.001 per day, since the nominal decline alone sets the timescale", "0.00035 per day, the product of the exponent and the nominal decline", "0.35 per day, the decline exponent used on its own"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The denominator is b times Di, which is 0.00035 per day. Dividing 1.15766927997459 by it gives 3307.62651421312 days. Forgetting the b in the denominator shrinks the answer by about a factor of three without producing anything that looks absurd.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 10, 'An analyst computes Ekene-1''s time to the 10 stb/d limit as log base 10 of 12 divided by 0.0012, rather than the natural log. The result is:', '["Unchanged, since the choice of logarithm base cancels out of the ratio of qi to the limit", "Acceptable only if the nominal decline is quoted per year instead of per day", "Too large by a factor of 12, matching the ratio of qi to the economic limit", "Too small by a factor of 2.30258509299405, with nothing about the units looking wrong"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The formula requires the natural log. Substituting base 10 divides the true 2070.75554149000 days by ln 10, which is 2.30258509299405, and the answer still comes out in plausible-looking days. Nothing in the output flags the error, which is why the habit of naming the base matters.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 11, 'Ekene-5''s time to the 10 stb/d limit is exactly 6000.00000000000 days. Which chain produces that?', '["The ratio 100/10 less one, divided by 0.0015", "The natural log of 100/10, divided by 0.0015, which is the exponential form", "qi divided by Di, giving the harmonic timescale directly", "The difference of 100 and 10, divided by 0.0015, borrowing the shape of the exponential EUR"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The harmonic time to limit is (qi/qLimit - 1)/Di, so 9 divided by 0.0015 gives 6000 days with no rounding anywhere. Check it from the other side: 100/(1 + 0.0015 times 6000) is exactly 10 stb/d.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 12, 'Ekene-1''s time to limit is 2070.75554149000 using Di per day, and 5.67330285339726 using the annual nominal 0.438. How do the two relate?', '["They differ by the tangent effective annual decline of 35.4674217142705 percent", "They are one answer in two units, days and years, because t inherits the units of Di", "The second is the time to a different limit, since an annual decline implies a different abandonment rate", "The second is wrong, because Arps time formulas require daily units throughout"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The units of t follow the units of Di and nothing else, and 2070.75554149000 divided by 365 is 5.67330285339726. Both are correct; the error only appears when one of them is written into a field labelled with the other unit.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 13, 'Which check confirms that an EUR and a time to limit were computed consistently?', '["The EUR divided by the time to limit must come back equal to qi", "The rate at the time to limit must equal half of the initial rate qi", "The model''s cumulative, evaluated at the time to limit, must equal the EUR", "The EUR must be smaller than the ratio qi/Di for every one of the three models"]'::jsonb, 2, 'EUR is by definition the cumulative at the moment the rate reaches the limit, so the two closed forms must agree. On Ekene-5, the harmonic cumulative at 6000 days is 66666.6666666667 times ln 10, which is 153505.672866270 stb, the EUR exactly. Comparing against qi/Di is no use on a harmonic well, which has no ceiling.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 14, 'Moving Ekene-5''s limit from 10 to 5 stb/d adds 30.1029995663981 percent to its booking, and moving it from 10 to 20 stb/d removes the same 30.1029995663981 percent. Why is the swing symmetric?', '["Ekene-5''s qi of 100 stb/d happens to be an exact multiple of both of those limits", "The harmonic model is the only Arps model whose cumulative is linear in the limit rate", "Its EUR carries ln(qi/qLimit), so each halving of the limit adds the same 46209.8120373297 stb", "The symmetry is a coincidence of these limits and fails at other pairs of values"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A logarithm turns each halving of the limit into the same additive block, (qi/Di) ln 2, which is 46209.8120373297 stb for Ekene-5. The band from 20 down to 10 stb/d and the band from 10 down to 5 stb/d hold that identical volume, and the pattern repeats for every further halving.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-eur-and-the-limit', 15, 'Ekene-6, with b of 0.35, shows a larger limit sensitivity than Ekene-3, with b of 0.5: a spread of 29.3862711104582 percent of its own base booking against 24.6122826336598 percent. What does that show?', '["Ekene-6''s fit is unreliable, because its R2 sits below the Excellent band that starts at 0.95", "The two reported b values must be wrong, since sensitivity always rises monotonically with b", "Hyperbolic wells with b under 0.5 always outswing those with b above 0.5 at any limit", "Sensitivity depends on qi, Di, b and the limit together, so it has to be computed rather than inferred"]'::jsonb, 3, 'What drives the swing is how much of the booking sits in the low-rate band the limit is moving through. Ekene-6 holds 18893.4881723416 stb between 20 and 10 stb/d against Ekene-3''s 16042.4222884424 stb, and both fits recover their planted parameters with R2 of 1.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 1, 'In the Associate workflow, choosing the fit window comes before fitting. Why does the order matter?', '["The window states which regime you believe the well is in, and fitting first turns it into a knob", "The engine''s fit routine cannot apply a window once a fit result object has been created and cached", "Auto-select can only compare model families on data that has already been trimmed to a single window", "R2 is undefined over a full production history"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Decline analysis assumes unchanging conditions, so the window is decided from what you know about the field before any curve is fitted. Turning it into something you adjust after seeing the result destroys the meaning of every statistic that follows.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 2, 'Step 1 of the workflow says to record three things before a model is touched. Which set?', '["The rate units and cadence, the on-stream date, and the events that changed operating conditions", "The reservoir''s original oil in place, its bubble point pressure and its net pay thickness from the log analysis", "The fitted qi, Di and b values carried over from the previous year''s booking", "The economic limit, the discount rate and the price deck used for the case"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Units, start date and field events are part of the booking rather than background trivia. For Ekene-1 they are stb/d read at the start of each month, on stream 2020-01-01, and a waterflood beginning 2023-01-01, and it is that event which sets the window.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 3, 'An analyst nudges the window start forward one month at a time until R2 crosses 0.95, then reports that fit. The problem is:', '["An R2 of 0.95 is too weak a threshold to support a reserves booking on a producing asset", "The window was tuned to move a statistic, so the statistic stops meaning anything", "Monthly boundaries are too coarse, and a window must begin on the day the rate change occurred", "Auto-select should have been locked to a single model family before the window was moved at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A goodness-of-fit statistic only reports how well a curve describes data that was chosen on independent grounds. Once the data is selected to maximise the statistic, the statistic is measuring the search rather than the well.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 4, 'A fitted curve tracks the middle of the data and misses both the earliest and the latest points, while R2 reads 0.96. What follows?', '["The rate data needs smoothing before refitting", "The fit is acceptable, since 0.96 sits in the Excellent band", "The model family is probably wrong, because a high R2 does not excuse a systematic mismatch in shape", "The limit should be raised until the late points fall outside the booking"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Systematic curvature in the residuals is a shape failure, and shape failures are invisible in a single aggregate number. The workflow puts the visual check after the fit precisely so that a comfortable R2 cannot carry a wrong model family through.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 5, 'Why does the workflow insist that an EUR is always reported with its economic limit beside it?', '["The limit is one of the inputs that fixes the fitted exponent b", "Reserves auditors require the economic limit to be disclosed for tax computation purposes in every jurisdiction", "The closed forms are undefined unless the limit is expressed in stock tank barrels per day rather than per month", "The same decline books different volumes at different limits, so the figure is not reproducible"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The limit is an input to the booking rather than a footnote on it. Without the limit a reader cannot reproduce the number or compare it with anyone else''s, which is why the capstone field itself carries the limit in its label.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 6, 'A booking reports ''decline 35.4674217142705 percent per year'' and nothing else. What is missing?', '["That is the tangent effective decline, and the closed forms consume the nominal 0.438 per year", "Nothing is missing, since effective and nominal declines are interchangeable inside the Arps closed forms", "The count of significant figures, which a reserves report caps at four", "The name of the well, without which the decline cannot be audited"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Effective and nominal are two conventions for one decline, and the Arps equations only accept the nominal form. Naming the convention alongside the value is what stops the next reader feeding an effective decline into a formula that expects a nominal one.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 7, 'A capstone field comes back failed, outside its stated tolerance. The most likely fix is:', '["Switching the panel from Auto-select to the specific model family you expected to see", "Rereading the lesson that derives that field, because the miss is a method error", "Resubmitting the same value carried to a great many more decimal places than before", "Recomputing the value from the engine''s daily forecast rather than from the closed form"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tolerances are set wide enough that any honest route to the number lands inside them, so rounding is almost never the cause. A failure outside tolerance means the method that produced the number was wrong.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 8, 'Submitting Ekene-1''s full-history fit instead of its primary-window fit fails several capstone fields at once. What does that reveal about the capstone?', '["It grades arithmetic precision, because the tolerances stated on the six graded fields are extremely tight", "It grades the window decision, because qi, Di, the EUR and the time to limit all descend from it", "It grades familiarity with the panel controls far more than it grades any engineering judgment at all", "It grades the economic limit, which a full-history fit silently changes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Four of the six graded fields are downstream of the fitted parameters, and the fitted parameters are downstream of the window. One judgment made before any arithmetic decides most of the score.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 9, 'A fit returns b sitting exactly on 1.95, the top of the engine''s search range. That is best read as:', '["A rounding artefact of the engine''s 0.05 grid steps", "A high-quality hyperbolic result, since a large b indicates strong pressure support in the reservoir", "A sign the model does not fit, since the search wanted to keep going and was stopped", "Evidence the well is moving from hyperbolic towards exponential decline"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A parameter resting on a boundary means the optimizer was still improving when it ran out of room. Read as a diagnostic it says the chosen model family cannot describe this data, which on Ekene-1''s full history is the flood response being forced into a primary decline.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 10, 'Ekene-1''s full-history fit returns qi of 97.2058663778433 stb/d, while the first recorded monthly rate is 120 stb/d. What does the gap say?', '["The well was choked back during its first month on production", "The first month''s rate is an outlier and should be removed", "The engine normalizes qi to the mean rate of the fitted window", "The fitted curve cannot reach the early data, which is a window problem rather than a data problem"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A fitted initial rate that undershoots the earliest observed rate means the curve is being pulled by data from a different regime, here the flood era. The check takes seconds and it points at the window, not at the early measurements.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 11, 'Ekene-1''s annual nominal decline is 0.438 per year. Put into the exponential EUR formula alongside daily rates it returns 251.141552511416 stb. What went wrong?', '["The decline and the rates were in different time units, so the answer is out by a factor of 365", "The economic limit was left out of the numerator of the exponential closed form when it was entered", "The nominal decline was used in the slot where the tangent effective value 0.354674217142705 belongs", "The exponential form is invalid once the nominal decline rises above 0.1"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The formula divides a rate difference in stb/d by a decline that must also be per day. Using the annual figure shrinks the booking by exactly 365, from 91666.6666666667 stb down to 251.141552511416 stb, which is absurd enough that this version of the error usually gets caught.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 12, 'The workflow starts from the rate table for one well and one stream. Fitting a gross liquid rate table and booking the result as an oil EUR would have what effect?', '["It stays acceptable as long as the well''s water cut remains somewhere below 50 percent", "It books water as though it were oil, since the decline describes the wrong stream", "It changes only the fitted qi, leaving the nominal decline and the exponent untouched by it", "It gets rejected automatically by auto-select, which accepts only oil rate streams as input"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A decline curve knows nothing about what is in the stream you handed it. Fit the stream you intend to book and state which stream it was, because oil and gross liquid decline differently once a flood is supporting the well.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 13, 'Substituting a tangent effective annual decline into a closed form that expects the nominal decline:', '["Produces a negative EUR whenever the effective decline exceeds 30 percent per year", "Understates the booking, since effective declines are always the larger of the two", "Overstates the booking by 23.4936115538863 percent for Ekene-1", "Leaves the EUR unchanged but shifts the time to limit by a factor of 365"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The effective decline is always the smaller number, 0.354674217142705 against 0.438 for Ekene-1, and EUR scales as one over the decline, so the ratio 1.23493611553886 inflates the booking. It is a units error wearing a percent sign, small enough to pass as a difference of opinion.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 14, 'Cumulative production found by multiplying each month''s opening rate by the days in that month, then summing, gives 74502.9269694921 stb for Ekene-1''s primary window against an exact 73157.9366256283 stb. What is the nature of the error?', '["A rounding artefact of the fifteen-digit arithmetic used in the closed form", "Random scatter from monthly reporting that averages out over a long enough production history", "A one-sided bias that accumulates, since the opening rate is the highest rate of each month", "An understatement, since holding one rate across a month discards the decline within it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For a declining well the opening rate of a month is that month''s maximum, so holding it for the whole month credits barrels that were never produced, once a month for three years. The 1344.99034386380 stb overstatement is more than three times the 400 stb tolerance the capstone allows on that field.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-booking', 15, 'The Professional tier takes up type curves, where decline character is borrowed from wells that already have history. Which trap does it exist to teach?', '["Normalizing rate and time removes the need to choose a fit window", "Type curves cannot be fitted unless every pooled well shares the same on-stream date", "A pooled fit returns the arithmetic average of the pooled wells'' exponents", "A borrowed curve can match a well''s history closely and still book a materially wrong volume"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Goodness of fit is measured over history, while the borrowed exponent does its real work in the tail that the history never observed. That is why an excellent match can sit alongside a badly wrong booking, and why the analogy between wells has to be argued rather than assumed.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'During primary depletion, the fundamental reason a producer''s oil rate declines is:', '["Reservoir pressure falls as fluid is withdrawn, shrinking the drawdown", "The rock''s permeability degrades steadily with production time", "Water cut from the injection pattern climbs and progressively displaces oil in the tubing string", "The wellbore radius is reduced by scale until the well dies"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Primary decline is an energy story: withdrawing fluid depletes reservoir pressure, and the shrinking drawdown delivers less rate. Rising water cut from injection belongs to the flood era, not primary depletion, and permeability does not decay simply because time passes. The drawdown that drives flow into the wellbore shrinks as the reservoir depletes.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'The Ekene teaching rates are deliberately noise-free. What does that mean in practice?', '["The monthly rates were smoothed with a trailing three-month moving average before they were committed to the fixture", "Every rate sits exactly on its planted Arps curve", "The engine filters measurement noise automatically during fitting", "Rates below the economic limit were removed from the table"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture generates each monthly rate exactly from the planted model, with no added scatter. That is why correct fits return R2 of 1 and why closed-form hand calculations land on the same numbers the engine reports. Nothing was smoothed or filtered. A correct fit therefore recovers the planted parameters exactly, and every graded number is reachable by hand.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'In the Ekene field story, what happened to wells Ekene-2 and Ekene-4?', '["They are the field''s two hyperbolic producers", "They were drilled after the flood began and never produced", "They found the sand wet and became water injectors", "They watered out during primary depletion in 2022 and were plugged and abandoned before the flood began"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-2 and Ekene-4 penetrated TOP_SAND below the 1560 m contact and found it wet. Dry on oil, they became the flood''s injectors from 2023-01-01. The two hyperbolic producers are Ekene-3 and Ekene-6. Both penetrated TOP_SAND below the 1560 m oil-water contact, produced no oil, and were converted to injectors at the flood start.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Why does the primary fit window for every Ekene producer end at the flood start, 2023-01-01?', '["The rate table stops reporting monthly rows on that date", "Injection changed the operating conditions Arps assumes are constant", "All four wells reached the economic limit that month", "The reporting cadence switched from monthly to daily rows on that date, changing how the fit weights the data"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Arps decline analysis is only valid while the drainage conditions that created the decline persist. Injection support is a new condition, so data after the flood start describes a different regime. The table itself continues through 2025-12-01. Decline analysis is only valid while the drive mechanism and operating conditions hold steady.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Ekene-1''s fitted nominal decline is Di = 0.0012 per day. Expressed as a nominal annual decline this is:', '["0.438 per year", "0.354674 per year", "0.0012 per year", "0.518091 per year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nominal declines scale linearly with the time unit: 0.0012 per day times 365 days gives 0.438 per year. The value 0.354674 is Ekene-1''s tangent effective annual decline, a different quantity, and 0.518091 is Ekene-3''s tangent effective decline.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Ekene''s producers came online on different dates. When the engine fits one well, time t in the Arps equation is measured from:', '["The field''s production start, 2020-01-01, for every well", "The flood start, so all wells share a common zero", "The date of the well''s peak monthly volume", "The first data row inside that well''s fit window, the fit''s own t0"]'::jsonb, 3, 'fitArpsModel sets t0 to the earliest row that survives the window filter and counts days from there. Wells that started in March, June or September 2020 each get their own zero; forcing a shared field-wide zero would shift every fitted qi.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'A row in the Ekene monthly rate table, such as 96.4565 for Ekene-1 on 2020-07-01, represents:', '["The volume produced during that calendar month, in stb", "The instantaneous oil rate on the first of the month, in stb/d", "The average oil rate over the whole month, in stb/d", "Cumulative production from startup to that date, in stb"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture samples the planted curve exactly on the first of each month, so each row is a spot rate in stb/d. Volumes only appear when a convention is applied, and the course shows how the choice of convention moves the cumulative.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Which expression is the Arps exponential rate-time form?', '["q = qi (1 - Di t)", "q = qi / (1 + Di t)", "q = qi exp(-Di t)", "q = qi / (1 + b Di t)^(1/b)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Exponential decline is q = qi exp(-Di t). The form with (1 + Di t) in the denominator is harmonic, and the general power form is the hyperbolic family that contains both as limits.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'On a semilog plot with log rate against linear time, an exponential decline appears as:', '["A straight line", "A curve that is concave up and keeps flattening", "A parabola opening downward", "A straight line only after water breakthrough"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Taking the logarithm of q = qi exp(-Di t) gives ln q = ln qi minus Di t, a straight line with slope set by Di. Hyperbolic and harmonic declines flatten on the same axes, which is exactly how the plot helps discriminate.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'In the hyperbolic family, the exponent b controls:', '["The initial rate the curve starts from", "The conversion between the daily Di the engine fits and the annual decline that gets reported", "The economic limit applied to the forecast", "How fast the instantaneous decline itself weakens"]'::jsonb, 3, 'b sets the curvature: at b = 0 the decline rate stays constant, and the larger b is, the faster the instantaneous decline relaxes, stretching the tail. qi sets the starting rate and the economic limit is a booking choice, not a curve parameter. At b = 0 the decline never weakens; the larger b is, the faster it fades and the fatter the tail.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'Setting b = 0 and b = 1 in the hyperbolic family gives, respectively:', '["Harmonic and exponential decline", "Exponential and harmonic decline", "Exponential and linear decline", "Harmonic and hyperbolic decline"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The hyperbolic form collapses to the exponential when b goes to 0 and becomes the harmonic when b equals 1. The two named end members bracket the family the same way Ekene-1 and Ekene-5 bracket the field''s producers.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Ekene-1 declines at Di = 0.0012 per day. Its tangent effective annual decline, 1 - exp(-365 Di), is:', '["43.80 percent", "42.16 percent", "35.47 percent", "30.58 percent"]'::jsonb, 2, '1 - exp(-0.438) = 0.354674, so 35.47 percent of the rate is lost over a year. 43.80 percent is the nominal annual decline misread as effective, 42.16 percent belongs to Ekene-5 and 30.58 percent to Ekene-6.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'For a hyperbolic well such as Ekene-3, the SPEE secant effective annual decline is defined as:', '["1 - q(1 year)/qi on the well''s own rate form", "1 - exp(-365 Di), the tangent form, which SPEE applies to every model family alike", "Di multiplied by 365", "The slope read from the semilog straight line"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The secant definition compares the rate one year out against the initial rate using the model''s own curve, so for a hyperbolic well it depends on b as well as Di. The exponential formula 1 - exp(-365 Di) is the tangent definition, a different convention. The secant form reads the actual rate one year out, so it respects the hyperbolic or harmonic shape.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Ekene-3 (qi 150 stb/d, Di 0.002/d, b 0.5) flows 80.5056 stb/d after one year. Its secant effective annual decline is:', '["53.67 percent", "51.81 percent", "73.00 percent", "46.33 percent"]'::jsonb, 3, '1 - 80.5056/150 = 0.463296, so 46.33 percent. The figure 53.67 percent is the surviving rate fraction, 51.81 percent is the tangent effective decline for the same well, and 73.00 percent is the nominal annual decline.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'SPEE REP #6 Table 1 pairs a nominal decline of 10 percent per year with a tangent effective decline of:', '["10.536 percent", "9.516 percent", "10.000 percent", "9.091 percent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tangent effective value is 1 - exp(-0.10) = 9.516 percent. Going the other way, a 10 percent effective decline corresponds to a nominal of 10.536 percent, and 9.091 percent is the table''s secant value at b = 1.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'Cumulative production Np relates to the rate history as:', '["The initial rate minus the current rate", "The current rate multiplied by elapsed time", "The integral of rate over time", "The sum of the first-of-month spot rates"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Volume is the area under the rate curve, which is why each Arps model has a closed-form cumulative. Multiplying a single rate by elapsed time or summing spot rates are approximations whose errors the course quantifies.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Using the exponential cumulative (qi/Di)(1 - exp(-Di t)), Ekene-1''s Np after 365 days is:', '["35467.42 stb", "40109.89 stb", "29109.38 stb", "27706.76 stb"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With qi/Di = 100000 stb, the bracket is 1 - exp(-0.438) = 0.354674, giving 35467.42 stb. The other volumes are the one-year cumulatives of Ekene-3, Ekene-5 and Ekene-6 respectively.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'The harmonic cumulative is Np = (qi/Di) ln(1 + Di t). For Ekene-5 (qi 100 stb/d, Di 0.0015/d) at t = 1096 days this gives:', '["66666.67 stb", "58807.55 stb", "64819.53 stb", "49303.57 stb"]'::jsonb, 2, 'qi/Di is 66666.67 stb and ln(1 + 1.644) = 0.97229, so Np = 64819.53 stb. Quoting 66666.67 stb reports the multiplier itself, 58807.55 stb is Ekene-5''s cumulative at the flood start (945 days), and 49303.57 stb is its value at 730 days.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Summing rate-on-the-first times days-in-month across Ekene-1''s primary window gives 74502.93 stb against the exact 73157.94 stb. The snapshot sum overstates because:', '["Monthly reporting skips weekends, deflating the exact figure", "Each month is billed at its opening rate", "The engine adds solution gas volumes to the oil column", "Rounding each daily rate upward accumulates over 36 months"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Holding the first-of-month rate flat is a left-endpoint rectangle. On a declining curve the opening rate is the month''s maximum, so every rectangle sits above the true curve, overstating by 1.84 percent here. No gas or rounding is involved. On a declining curve the opening rate is the highest rate that month will see, so every month is overstated.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'The engine''s stop-at-limit daily forecast for Ekene-1 accumulates 91604.12 stb, slightly below the closed-form EUR of 91666.67 stb, because:', '["Right-endpoint daily rectangles understate the area under a declining curve", "It drops the first month of production from the sum", "Float32 accumulation in the daily loop loses the last two significant digits of the running total", "The economic limit is subtracted twice from the total"]'::jsonb, 0, 'generateForecast adds q(day) for day 1, 2, 3 and so on: each day is represented by its closing rate, the lowest rate that day sees. The bias is the mirror image of the monthly snapshot overstatement, and here it is 62.54 stb, about 0.07 percent. The engine sums the rate at the END of each day, so every rectangle sits below the true curve.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'Which cumulative convention does the Ekene material-balance survey history use for Np at each pressure survey?', '["Monthly snapshot sums, matching the rate table", "The engine''s daily rectangular sums", "An average of the snapshot and daily conventions", "The exact closed-form integral of each planted decline"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The survey cumulatives are the closed-form integrals summed across the four producers, which is why hand arithmetic from the Arps cumulative formulas reproduces them to the last digit. The approximate conventions exist in the course to be compared against it.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Ekene-1''s EUR at the 10 stb/d limit is 91666.67 stb and its cumulative at the flood start is 73157.94 stb. Had primary decline continued, the remaining recovery from the flood start would have been:', '["18508.73 stb", "91666.67 stb", "73157.94 stb", "16484.26 stb"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Remaining recovery is EUR minus cumulative to date: 91666.67 - 73157.94 = 18508.73 stb. EUR always counts from time zero, so quoting the full 91666.67 stb as remaining would double-count what the well has already produced.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'To fit an exponential decline, the engine performs a linear regression of:', '["Rate against time, then reads Di from the intercept", "ln(rate) against time, with slope -Di and intercept ln(qi)", "1/rate against time, with slope Di/qi", "rate^(-b) against time for a scanned b"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The exponential linearizes through the logarithm: ln q falls on a straight line whose slope gives Di and whose intercept gives qi. Regressing 1/q against t is the harmonic linearization, and the power transform belongs to the hyperbolic grid search.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'The harmonic model is fitted through a regression that is linear in:', '["ln(rate) versus time", "rate versus ln(time)", "1/rate versus time", "sqrt(rate) versus time"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Inverting q = qi/(1 + Di t) gives 1/q = 1/qi + (Di/qi) t, a straight line in 1/q. The log transform is the exponential''s linearization; neither of the other transforms produces a line for any Arps member.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'How does the engine determine the hyperbolic exponent b?', '["It scans b in 0.05 steps and keeps the lowest RMSE", "It solves for b by Newton iteration on the rate residuals, the method Ikoku''s published gas-well example uses", "It reads b directly from the curvature of the semilog plot", "It averages the b implied by the exponential and harmonic end members"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For each b on a 0.05 grid the engine linearizes q^(-b) against time, fits, and keeps the best. That is why a recovered b always sits on the grid: Ekene-6 lands exactly on 0.35 because the planted value is a grid point. Each candidate b gets its own linearized regression; the engine keeps whichever fit has the lowest RMSE.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'With modelType set to Auto-Select, the engine returns:', '["The hyperbolic candidate whenever one converges", "The candidate with the fewest fitted parameters", "The first candidate whose R2 exceeds 0.95", "The candidate model with the lowest RMSE"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Auto-Select fits every family it can and sorts the candidates by RMSE, returning the smallest. On the noise-free Ekene windows the planted family wins with residuals at machine precision, so auto-selection recovers the planted model every time.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'getFitQuality assigns a fit with R2 = 0.93 to which tier?', '["Excellent", "Good", "Fair", "Poor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The bands are Excellent at 0.95 and above, Good at 0.90, Fair at 0.80, and Poor below that. An R2 of 0.93 clears the Good threshold but not the Excellent one.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Forcing an EXPONENTIAL fit on Ekene-3''s hyperbolic primary data returns R2 = 0.9873, inside the Excellent band. The correct reading of that result is:', '["Ekene-3 is really an exponential well, and the planted b of 0.5 recorded in the fixture is a labelling error", "The quality bands are miscalibrated for monthly data", "A high R2 over a short window cannot by itself identify the model family", "Any R2 above 0.95 confirms the chosen model is physically correct"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Early in a decline the hyperbolic and exponential curves are nearly indistinguishable, so the wrong family can still score 0.9873. Model discrimination needs curve shape over time, rate context, or more history, not a single goodness-of-fit number. The exponent b is barely expressed early in a well''s life, so the families are nearly indistinguishable there.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'On the noise-free Ekene primary windows, every correctly specified fit reports R2 = 1 because:', '["The data lie exactly on the planted curve", "The engine rounds any R2 above 0.99 up to 1", "Monthly sampling guarantees a perfect fit for any model", "R2 is defined to equal 1 whenever rates decrease monotonically"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With zero noise the fitted curve passes through every point and the residual sum is on the order of 1e-14. The engine does no rounding, and the wrong model on the same data scores visibly below 1, which is how the fixture separates truth from convenience. Residuals sit at machine precision, which is why RMSE comes back around 1e-14 rather than zero.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'The economic limit rate used in an EUR booking is:', '["The lowest rate the metering system can resolve", "The rate at which reservoir pressure falls to the bubble point and solution gas begins to break out", "A regulatory minimum set by the licensing authority", "The rate below which operating the well no longer pays"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The limit is an economic statement: barrels below it cost more to lift than they earn, so they are excluded from EUR. It is chosen by the operator''s economics, not by metering physics, fluid behavior or regulation. It is a commercial input, and it is where the recovery integration stops.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'The exponential EUR to a limit is (qi - qlim)/Di. For Ekene-1 to 10 stb/d this books:', '["95833.33 stb", "91666.67 stb", "100000.00 stb", "83333.33 stb"]'::jsonb, 1, '(120 - 10)/0.0012 = 91666.67 stb. Dropping the limit to 5 stb/d gives 95833.33, raising it to 20 gives 83333.33, and 100000 is qi/Di, the volume the well would deliver if produced forever to zero rate.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'The time for Ekene-1 to decline from 120 to 10 stb/d, ln(qi/qlim)/Di, is:', '["2648.38 days", "2872.98 days", "2070.76 days", "1493.13 days"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ln(12)/0.0012 = 2070.76 days, about 5.7 years. Waiting to 5 stb/d takes 2648.38 days, cutting off at 20 stb/d takes 1493.13, and 2872.98 days is Ekene-3''s time to the 10 stb/d limit.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'Halving Ekene-1''s limit from 10 to 5 stb/d adds 4166.67 stb of EUR but 577.62 days of producing life. The general lesson is:', '["The last barrels are slow: little volume for a lot of time", "EUR is essentially insensitive to the economic limit once a well is past its first year", "Time to limit falls whenever the limit falls", "Lowering the limit reduces the booked EUR"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Near the limit the well produces at its slowest, so each extra barrel takes longer than any before it. The EUR gain from 91666.67 to 95833.33 stb costs the time from 2070.76 to 2648.38 days, a poor trade that operating cost usually forbids. Lowering the limit buys 4.5 percent more EUR for 28 percent more producing life.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Ekene-5 (harmonic) needs exactly 6000 days to reach 10 stb/d while Ekene-1 (exponential) needs 2070.76. The heavy harmonic tail exists because:', '["Ekene-5 started with a higher initial rate", "At b = 1 the instantaneous decline keeps weakening", "Ekene-5''s nominal Di of 0.0015 per day is gentler than Ekene-1''s 0.0012 per day", "Harmonic behavior indicates wellbore damage that slows flow"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The harmonic decline rate relaxes in proportion to rate, stretching the approach to any limit. Ekene-5 actually starts LOWER than Ekene-1 (100 against 120 stb/d) and its Di is larger, yet the b = 1 tail still nearly triples the producing life. Because the decline itself fades, the rate falls ever more slowly and the tail stretches out.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Ekene-6 (qi 90 stb/d, Di 0.001/d, b 0.35): its EUR at a 20 stb/d economic limit is:', '["105266.63 stb", "117307.07 stb", "86373.14 stb", "95227.74 stb"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The hyperbolic EUR formula at qlim 20 gives 86373.14 stb for Ekene-6. The values 105266.63 and 117307.07 are the same well''s bookings at limits of 10 and 5 stb/d, and 95227.74 stb is Ekene-3''s booking at 20 stb/d.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'At the common 10 stb/d limit, which Ekene producer books the LARGEST closed-form EUR?', '["Ekene-1, because it has the highest EUR-to-qi ratio", "Ekene-3, because it starts at the highest rate", "Ekene-6, because its Di is the smallest", "Ekene-5, because the harmonic tail keeps delivering volume for 6000 days"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-5 books 153505.67 stb, the field''s largest, despite the lowest qi of the four. The harmonic tail is the reason: b controls the tail, and the tail is where late-life volume lives. Starting rate alone is a poor predictor of EUR.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Put the Associate booking workflow in the right order:', '["Book EUR first, then fit the model, then choose the window", "Window the data, fit and check quality, then book EUR and time to limit at the stated economic limit", "Fit the full history, then window, then report R2", "Check R2 on last year''s data, then extrapolate by eye"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The window comes first because it defines which physics the fit describes; the fit and its quality check come next; the booking numbers are computed last, from the fitted parameters at the stated limit. Every other ordering lets an invalid fit reach the booking.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'An analyst fits Di = 0.0012 per day but labels it 0.0012 per year in the booking memo. An EUR computed downstream from (qi - qlim)/Di using the mislabeled value would be:', '["Understated by a factor of 365", "Unchanged, because EUR does not depend on time units", "Overstated by a factor of 365", "Wrong by exp(365)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'EUR divides by Di, so treating a per-day decline as per-year makes the divisor 365 times too small and the volume 365 times too large. Units ride through every decline formula; the course states Di per day precisely to force this discipline.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'Fitting one Arps curve through Ekene-1''s FULL history, flood response included, is invalid because:', '["Injection changed the operating conditions mid-history, so no single constant-conditions decline describes the whole record", "The engine cannot process data recorded after 2023", "There are too many data points for a stable regression", "Monthly sampling is too sparse after the flood begins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The record spans two regimes: primary depletion, then injection-supported production with a response ramp. A single Arps curve describes neither, even though the engine will happily return parameters and a plausible-looking R2 for the blend.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'The capstone grades each answer inside a stated tolerance, for example the EUR within 500 stb. Why a tolerance rather than an exact match?', '["The grading engine cannot compare full-precision numbers", "The expected answers change every time the fixture is regenerated", "Correct answers are drawn randomly from a range", "Honest reading and rounding paths differ in the last digits, and the tolerance accepts any correct method"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A learner may read a panel tile, compute the closed form by hand, or carry different rounding, all legitimately. Tolerances are set tight enough to reject wrong methods and loose enough to accept every correct one. The fixture and its expected values are fixed.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'For the booking memo, cumulative production to date should be taken from:', '["The monthly snapshot sum, since it is within 2 percent", "The closed-form integral of the fitted model, or the measured cumulative itself", "The peak rate multiplied by the days on production", "The engine''s daily sum, corrected upward by 0.07 percent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When the model is trusted, its closed-form cumulative is exact; when metered volumes exist, they are the record. The snapshot sum carries a known upward bias, peak-rate arithmetic is not a cumulative at all, and hand-correcting the daily sum papers over a method choice.'),
  ('dca', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Before submitting the Ekene-1 booking, the quickest self-check that fitted qi and Di are right is to:', '["Confirm the fit reports b equal to 1", "Read qi off the last row of the rate table", "Recompute the rate at a known date from the closed form and compare it to the table, for example q(365) = 77.4391 stb/d", "Check that R2 is above 0.80 and stop there"]'::jsonb, 2, 'One forward evaluation of q = qi exp(-Di t) against a table row catches swapped units, wrong t0 and wrong parameters at once. R2 alone cannot catch a units error, the last table row is the newest rate rather than qi, and b is 0 for this well.');
